# .gitleaksignore — reviewed, commit-scoped exceptions for the nightly
# full-history audit (`scripts/gitleaks-ci.sh <version> history`).
#
# Format (see gitleaks detect/detect.go, AddGitleaksIgnore):
#   file:rule-id:start-line                     global  — ignored in EVERY commit
#   commit:file:rule-id:start-line              scoped  — ignored only in that commit
# Only the scoped form is used here on purpose: a global entry would also silence
# the same file and line in future commits, which is exactly where a real secret
# would appear next.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accepted 2026-11-15 — Supabase PUBLISHABLE (anon) key committed in 70405ba.
#
#   .env.example:3            VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_...
#   src/lib/supabase.ts:4     const FALLBACK_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY = 'sb_publishable_...'
#
# Why this is not treated as a breach: a Supabase publishable key is public by
# design. It ships inside every browser bundle, identifies the project, and
# grants nothing beyond what row-level security allows — this project verifies
# 103 RLS policies with 0 tables missing RLS, and the privileged service-role key
# lives only in Edge Function secrets (`SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`), never in the
# client. `scripts/security-check.mjs` blocks any service-role key or JWT from
# being committed, and that gate passes on every run.
#
# Both lines are already gone from the current tree: `.env.example` ships empty
# values, and `src/lib/supabase.ts` has no hard-coded fallback — an absent or
# invalid key puts the app into Offline-Edge mode instead.
#
# Still recommended: rotate the publishable key in the Supabase dashboard if you
# want the historical value to stop working. Rotating is cheap (update
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY wherever the app is deployed); rewriting git history is
# not, and is not warranted for a value that was never secret.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
